faq:
  - q: "What is agentic engineering?"
    a: "Agentic engineering is a way of building software in which AI agents do a large share of the implementation inside a defined workflow, while senior engineers own the definition, review and release of every change."
  - q: "What's the difference between AI-assisted, AI-augmented, and agentic engineering?"
    a: "They're three rungs of a ladder. AI-assisted means individual engineers use copilots. AI-augmented means the team's workflow is redesigned around AI. Agentic engineering means agents implement inside governed loops, and senior engineers own judgment: the specification, the review and the release."
  - q: "How does an engineering team adopt AI without losing quality?"
    a: "Start with one real workflow, not a tool rollout. We write the specification before the prompt, wire agents into the work with tests and review guardrails in CI/CD, and measure time to production and change frequency against your current baseline. Then we widen it to the next workflow."
  - q: "How do you keep AI-written code maintainable?"
    a: "By building the system around the code for it. Agents implement inside a defined workflow, a senior engineer reviews every change before it ships, and the tests and conventions stay strong enough that the next change is easy too. If the pipeline is slow or the tests are thin, a faster team just ships more risk."
  - q: "Does agentic engineering replace our engineers?"
    a: "No. We work alongside your team. The specifications, guardrails and review habits we build stay with your engineers, and agents take on more of the execution while your people own more of the judgment."
  - q: "How do engagements work and how do you bill?"
    a: "Agentic Engineering usually starts as a fractional, high-throughput engagement: senior experts working 15 to 20 hours a week alongside your leads. Engineers can also join full time through Talent Augmentation. Engagements bill time and materials."
